<https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-hentschel-tgfb-fibronectin-limb-specimen-match> a pk:LLMClaimAssessment ;
    rdfs:label "Hentschel, Glimm, Glazier & Newman (2004) Proc. R. Soc. B / Kiskowski et al. (2004) Dev. Biol. multiscale TGF-beta / FGF-inhibitor reaction-diffusion and fibronectin haptotaxis PDE in developing Gallus gallus limb bud, coupled to a Glazier-Graner-Hogeweg cellular Potts cell lattice — Morphological realism / specimen match: 76/100" ;
    dct:date "2026-10-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:description "Credit: Panel E/F still segment the SHA-256-locked Gallus wing into 1 / 2 / 3 elements (L1027-1084) and Panel H compares theory, PDE and specimen counts; Panel I shows discrete GGH cells visibly condensing inside the continuum Hill contour and Panel J shows compact discrete zeugopod (2) and autopod (3) condensations at the digit positions, which is the first cell-level morphology in this loop. Deductions: the 1D and 2D wing \"condensations\" in Panels B and C are one-pixel-wide clip spikes rather than the several-cell-wide condensations of Hentschel Figs. 2-4 or the specimen's bone widths (-8); the micromass morphospace (Panel D) no longer shows a spot vs stripe distinction — both regimes are labyrinthine bands, so the Miura & Shiota (2000) nodular-island phenotype in the dataset is not reproduced (-6); the GGH wing bud forms no stylopod condensation (Panel J: GGH m = 0 vs PDE m = 1 vs specimen 1 humerus) (-4); no side-by-side with a Kiskowski (2004) or Chaturvedi (2005) cell-lattice figure (-3); the GGH sheet dewets into medium holes (Panel I) that have no counterpart in a confluent micromass (-3)." ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/concept/morphoeval-0.1.0-specimen-match> ;
